close_reason: "Replaced the same-name delete branch in shellScalars with lineScalarBinding, a per-line persistence walk verified against bash for all fourteen persistence shapes. Both halves of the finding were measured as live bypasses before the fix (final-value discard: failures 0 over an unattested $NPM publish; pipeline/background indexing: failures 0 over an unflagged $FLAG publish) and both are closed."
body: "Greptile P1, security, scripts/shell-command-scan.ts:737-740 (PR 84):\n\n\"**Reassignment discards final scalar** — When a line contains persistent assignments such as `NPM=ignored; NPM=npm`, this branch deletes `NPM` instead of retaining its final value. A later `\\$NPM publish` therefore remains unresolved and is omitted from the audit, allowing an attested sibling to produce a clean result despite the unattested publish. **How this was verified:** The remainder check deletes any same-name scalar, while expansion leaves missing scalars unresolved and publish recognition consequently does not classify \\$NPM as npm.\"\n\nGreptile's 2026-08-28 summary (confidence 1/5) named the same error class from the other direction, and the maintainer asked for it to be re-examined rather than assumed resolved: \"it treats pipeline or background assignments as file-wide bindings; either behavior can make static expansion diverge from the shell and conceal missing provenance\" — i.e. \"pipeline or background assignments are retained as global provenance evidence, while final same-name reassignments are discarded.\"\n\nCodeRabbit's guidance for the fix: \"The reassignment fix should preserve the last persistent, file-scope assignment. It must not promote pipeline, background, or command-prefix assignments into global scalar state.\""
